While testing with doppler, I noticed it tended to keep higher pitch more than lower pitch. While looking to see if there was an better way to blend pitches, I realized there's a bug: `actual_pitch_scale` can never be less than `internal->pitch_scale` because of the `MAX`. So you never get low pitches for receding sounds, even in the single camera case.

I think I came up with a better way of calculating the actual pitch, using a weighted average. Since humans here pitch roughly in log2 space, the idea is to keep weighted log2 pitch scales, then normalize and change back to linear at the end.

```
	// set picth to default value
	// this will only change if the Doppler effect is activated
	actual_pitch_scale = internal->pitch_scale;
	float log_pitch_scale = 0.0;
	float log_pitch_weight = 0.0;
```

Then in the doppler section, instead of `MAX` you do:
```
	float doppler_pitch_scale = internal->pitch_scale * speed_of_sound / (speed_of_sound + velocity * approaching);
	doppler_pitch_scale = CLAMP(doppler_pitch_scale, (1 / 8.0), 8.0); //avoid crazy stuff

	float weight = _get_max_volume(tmp_volume_vector);
	log_pitch_scale += weight * log2(doppler_pitch_scale);
	log_pitch_weight += weight;
```

Where `_get_max_volume` just gets the largest volume response of any channel:
```
static float _get_max_volume(const Vector<AudioFrame> &p_src_volume_vector) {
	float max_vol = 0.0;
	for (int64_t i = 0; i < p_src_volume_vector.size(); i++) {
		max_vol = MAX(max_vol, p_src_volume_vector[i].left);
		max_vol = MAX(max_vol, p_src_volume_vector[i].right);
	}
	return max_vol;
}
```

After the cameras loop:
```
	if (log_pitch_weight > 0) {
		actual_pitch_scale = pow(2.0, log_pitch_scale / log_pitch_weight);
