So there are various other elements that handle this case and they do so similarly (though like I said, they special-case joystick which I find less clean than the above, but you may disagree and I'm happy to rewrite it).

**Example 1: Focus switching**: [viewport.cpp](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/df6235838b63dd49f448547406e339848455c140/scene/main/viewport.cpp#L2362):

```cpp
if (p_event->is_action_pressed(SNAME("ui_down")) && input->is_action_just_pressed_by_event(SNAME("ui_down"), p_event)) {
	next = from->_get_focus_neighbor(SIDE_BOTTOM);
	show_focus = true;
}
```

Note that this code has an entire block dedicated to joypad motion events, and then a separate block for other events, which handles echo and does not handle "just_pressed". I don't really see the point of having the same algorithm written out twice when you can just say "just pressed or echo" like I did here.

**Example 2: OptionButton / PopupMenu**: [popup_menu.cpp](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/df6235838b63dd49f448547406e339848455c140/scene/gui/popup_menu.cpp#L570):

```cpp
if (p_event->is_action("ui_down", true) && p_event->is_pressed()) {
	if (is_joypad_event) {
		if (!input->is_action_just_pressed_by_event("ui_down", p_event, true)) {
			return;
		}
		joypad_event_process = true;
		set_process_internal(true);
	}
	...
```

This one handles both joy and key events in the same block of code, but it special-cases joypad events before checking `is_action_just_pressed_by_event`, which means it doesn't need to say "or is_echo". Maybe that's what you want? It feels more hacky to explicitly special-case joypad.

Like I said in the initial PR, I feel like having every individual UI element (not to mention all user code - if you want to avoid this problem in your own event handlers) do some variation on the above is not a robust approach. I've got thoughts about how to solve this in a bigger picture sense, but that's more of a godot-proposals thing so I'm trying to address this immediately obvious issue first.

